==Passport Card and EDL==
The [[United States passport card]] or a state-issued [[enhanced driver's license]] (EDL) can be used as an alternative to the passport booklet only when travelling to and from Canada, Mexico, Bermuda, Anguilla, Antigua and Barbuda, Aruba, The Bahamas, British Virgin Islands, Caribbean Netherlands, Cayman Islands, Dominica, Dominican Republic, Grenada, Jamaica, Montserrat, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Lucia, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines and Turks and Caico at maritime [[Port of entry|ports-of-entry]] or land border crossings.<ref>{{cite web | title=Why Get a Passport Card? 4 Benefits of Having One | url=https://www.goabroad.com/articles/why-get-a-passport-card | website=Goabroad.com | publisher=GoAbroad.com | access-date=27 February 2024}}</ref> Passport cards and EDLs are proof of U.S. citizenship, but they are not valid for international air travel; if traveling by air, a passport book is required.<ref>state.gov: [https://travel.state.gov/content/passports/english/passports/information/card.html Passport Card] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905084744/http://travel.state.gov/content/passports/english/passports/information/card.html |date=September 5, 2015 }}, access-date: June 6, 2015</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Enhanced Drivers Licenses: What Are They? | url=https://www.dhs.gov/enhanced-drivers-licenses-what-are-they | website=Dhs.gov | access-date=15 February 2026}}</ref>

==APEC Business Travel Card==
The [[APEC Business Travel Card]] (ABTC) is meant to facilitate travel for U.S. citizens engaged in verified business in the [[APEC]] region.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.cbp.gov/travel/trusted-traveler-programs/us-apec-business-travel-card-program-frequently-asked-questions2 | title=U.S. APEC Business Travel Card Program Frequently Asked Questions - U.S. Customs and Border Protection | access-date=May 19, 2016 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160507203450/https://www.cbp.gov/travel/trusted-traveler-programs/us-apec-business-travel-card-program-frequently-asked-questions2 | archive-date=May 7, 2016 | url-status=dead}}</ref>

The U.S. [[ABTC]] should enable access to a dedicated fast-track lane for expedited immigration processing at some participating foreign [[APEC]] member airports.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://travel.apec.org/abtc-summary.html | title=ABTC Summary - APEC Business Travel Card|access-date=April 2, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161019010749/http://travel.apec.org/abtc-summary.html|archive-date=October 19, 2016 | url-status=dead}}</ref>

U.S. APEC Business Travel Card holders may also use the Global Entry kiosks at participating airports upon their U.S. return.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.cbp.gov/travel/trusted-traveler-programs/global-entry/locations | title=Airports with Global Entry Kiosks|website=U.S. Customs and Border Protection|date=May 24, 2023}}</ref>

As the U.S. is a transitional member of the ABTC scheme, however, the U.S. APEC Business Travel Card cannot be used in lieu of a visa to enter an APEC member country.<ref>{{cite web | title=The Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation Business Travel Card | url=https://www.apec.org/groups/committee-on-trade-and-investment/business-mobility-group/abtc | website=APEC | access-date=December 2, 2021 | language=en | date=September 2021}}</ref> The program was initially set to expire on September 30, 2018, but the November 2017 signing of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation Business Travel Cards Act of 2017 (S. 504) ensured it would permanently remain.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/115th-congress/senate-bill/504 | title=S.504 - Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation Business Travel Cards Act of 2017 | work=[[Congress.gov]] | date=November 2, 2017 | access-date=August 12, 2019}}</ref>

==Consular protection of U.S. citizens abroad==
[[File:Diplomatic_missions_of_the_United_States.svg|thumb|800px|none|American diplomatic missions, including embassies (blue), interests sections, and other representations (light blue)]]

The United States has the second most diplomatic missions of any country in the world.{{citation needed|date=February 2024}} See also [[List of diplomatic missions of the United States]] and [[List of diplomatic missions in the United States]].

The [[Department of State]] regularly publishes [[travel warning]]s or travel alerts.

